{
  "version": 357,
  "released": "2026-09-25",
  "changelog": "v357: a full name outranks a shared first name \u2014 the owner's father's moments are his father's again. WHERE IT WAS SEEN: the owner's own vault (a scratch clone at framework v356). *\"Father's mission to New Zealand\"* (`node:80f419115b858c37a7b051f3`) and four more of his father's moments \u2014 *\"Father's tenure as bishop\"*, *\"Father's four years bedridden\"*, *\"Father's disclosure about painkiller addiction\"*, *\"Naming of father as biggest loss\"* \u2014 were published with the RAW subject `James Edwin Taylor`, so no birth could reach them and an age said about any of them had nothing to count from: *\"Father's four years bedridden\"* carried `age_without_birth_anchor`, and the mission's own card had been answered *\"19-21 years old\"* by a man who then had to add *\"He was 19-21 not me. I wasn't born yet.\"* and *\"his birthday is June 4th 1954\"*. THE DEFECT: `James Edwin Taylor` is his father's full name, but the roster spells his father `James Taylor` and holds four other rows bearing *James* \u2014 his grandfather `James Edwin Taylor Sr.` (differing only by the suffix), his son `James Everett Taylor`, his brother `Anthon James Taylor` (alias `James`), and a fifth row, `james`, that is not a person at all but an ALIAS ROW (`maps_to_focus: anthon-james-taylor`). No exact key matched, and the only rung that read inside a name was v335's bare-given-name census \u2014 about ONE word \u2014 so the full name fell to `no_candidate`. And the alias row, which v343 had taken out of the card path's `roster_index`, was still a person with a ref and a birthday in the fold's BINDING path (`axis_membership.roster_person_rows`, which the birth index, person-key index and family-tier index read). THE RULES: (1) A FULL NAME OUTRANKS A SHARED FIRST NAME (`identity_resolution.A_FULL_NAME_OUTRANKS_A_SHARED_FIRST_NAME`, `full_name_candidates`, the new reason `full_name`): a mention whose name words carry one roster person's own spelling \u2014 given name and surname both anchored, every word in order, optionally with a middle name the roster does not spell \u2014 binds that person when exactly one does, read before the relationship rung and uniqueness-gated like every other rung; the spellings come from the roster itself (`RosterIndex.full_names`, from each name, slug and alias with determiners and relationship words dropped), never from counting a mention's words, and a mention is never allowed to reach INTO a longer roster name. A relationship word beside it is a veto only (`my son James Edwin Taylor` is not his father). (2) A GENERATIONAL SUFFIX IS ONE GENERATION, NOT A TIE (`A_GENERATIONAL_SUFFIX_IS_ONE_GENERATION`): a spelling's generation is part of the name, so `James Edwin Taylor` is never `James Edwin Taylor Sr.`, and `Sr.`/`Snr`/`Senior`/`I` and `Jr.`/`Jnr`/`II`/`III` all read off ONE table \u2014 v347's `GENERATIONAL_SUFFIX_STEPS`, moved to `identity_resolution` (the pure module both readers can import) and re-exported by `roster_relations` as the same object, with `snr`, `jnr`, `ii` and `iii` added for both readers at once. (3) AN ALIAS ROW IS NEVER A CANDIDATE ANYWHERE (`AN_ALIAS_ROW_IS_NEVER_A_CANDIDATE`, `is_alias_row`): `roster_person_rows` drops it, so it is never offered, counted, bound or used as an age anchor, in the binding path as in the card path. (4) v335 HOLDS: a bare *James* still binds nobody and still asks, naming the real people and never the pointer row \u2014 and because the pointer row was, by accident, the only thing keeping the word `james` out of the fold's birth index (two rows disagreeing), `temporal_timeline._person_key_index` now also treats a bare given name several people answer to as ambiguous (`shared_name_token_refs`, v335's own census), so *\"James's duck-chasing rowboat antics\"* does not quietly take his brother's 1990 birthday while its identity card is still open. MEASURED on the scratch clone, v356 \u2192 v357, one deterministic publish each: 5 nodes changed subject, all five from `James Edwin Taylor` to `person/james-taylor`, none re-keyed (node count 1265 \u2192 1265, aliases 1028 \u2192 1028); `age_without_birth_anchor` 13 \u2192 12 (the bedridden node's claim); cards precision_gap 43 \u2192 43, contradiction 11 \u2192 12, identity_uncertain 1 \u2192 1 (the same card, *\"Which Anthon James Taylor or James Everett Taylor or James Edwin Taylor Sr. or James Taylor is 'James' here?\"*, 16 claims, unchanged); placement audit lost [] and drawn_at_an_alias []. ONE READING MOVED, AND IT IS A CLASSIFIER ERROR THIS RELEASE UNMASKED RATHER THAN MADE: *\"Father's four years bedridden\"* carries an `age` claim `22, 23` whose own evidence says `when_hint: when I was just off my mission, so like 22, 23` \u2014 the NARRATOR's age, which the classifier's own `timeline_resolution.reason` records (\"Age 22,23 describes the author, not James\") but which it filed against James anyway, against its own contract (`date.age` is the SUBJECT's age). While the subject was unresolved that age had no anchor and the resolver's 2003-07/2009-07 stood; now it measures from 1954-06-04 to 1977~ and the resolver's reading is kept as the alternate, surfaced as a contradiction card (\"around 1977 and July 2003\u2013July 2009\") rather than silently replaced. The audit's `moved` leg therefore names that one node. Reading `when_hint` for WHOSE age it is belongs to the extractor, not the fold (lifehug#415). THE MISSION: its subject is now `person/james-taylor`; its placement is unchanged at the resolver's 1973-06/1976-06, and it is `none`/`pre_birth` rather than `family`/`immediate_family_in_lifetime` because v344's rule 4 puts a moment wholly before the owner's 1981 birth off his axis whoever it is about. The *\"19-21 years old\"* answer still does not reach it: that reply's card (`work:b7525efb7ce0bb69be2d7129`) was closed by the 2026-09-23 resolver run, so v352's `place-answers` refuses it `card_not_published` (lifehug#415). Filed by hand on the scratch clone, the age now measures from his father's birth to 1973-06-04/1976-06-03 and joins the resolver's reading as agreeing evidence \u2014 the arithmetic the resolver had done by hand. `CALCULATION_RULE_VERSION` MOVES to `timeline-rules:18`: the arithmetic is unchanged, but the same claims now resolve to a different subject set, and so to different age anchors and a different work-item set for a head nobody edited; the eleven pinned tests and `tests/goldens/cert_10_place_containment.json` move with it, and v347's step-set pin grows by `-2` for `III`. GUARDS: `tests/test_v357_a_full_name_outranks_a_shared_first_name.py` (26 tests) with the five real roster rows as fixtures, each rule SEEN failing with its guard removed first \u2014 including an AST sweep that fails on any second `sr`/`jr` table in `system/`. `tests/test_identity_resolution.py`'s closed reason-vocabulary pin gains `full_name` in ladder order. Full local suite: 6873 tests, 1 failure (the known macOS-only `test_file_answer_outer_queue_leaves_no_plaintext_temp`, which fails identically on a pristine v356 tree), 4 skipped. Ruff parity held: no new finding class in any touched module; the new test file carries only `RUF100`, the class its siblings carry. PLATFORM: pin the package \u2014 the resolution reason vocabulary gained `full_name` and the fold's rule version moved.",
